WEBVTT

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.200
This is Coast!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.480
At the edge of our isles is a natural wilderness.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:34.360
SEAGULLS Vast seas, deep oceans, tempestuous tides.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:49.080
The relationship between us and our wild waters is a challenge.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.080
But one we embrace.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:01:00.840
For work... ..play... ..and sheer necessity.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:09.200
There's nowhere wilder then our open waters, and for me, there's no bigger adrenaline rush.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:19.480
But out here, you're at the mercy of some seriously majestic natural forces, which can make the sea a perilous place to be.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:30.280
As I head for one of our most treacherous stretches, Northumberland's north-east, my fellow coast crew are also taking to the high seas.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.520
Walk on the boat!

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:39.160
Mark's exploring how a stalwart of our oceans, the roll-on, roll-off ferry, stays safe in wild waters.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.520
If the water from here gets into here, it could be lethal.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:53.840
Miranda's diving into the untamed depths of Dublin Bay.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.360
Out there is Britain's most venomous jellyfish, the Lion's Mane.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.640
And I'm about to enter its lair.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:06.960
And Dick's on the trail of a plan to make wartime waters even wilder... ..with fire.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:07.320
The water's bubbling.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:07.760
Yep.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.680
We're boiling the sea?

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.479
Yes.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.080
Welcome to the wild waters of our coast.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:15.960
OK, Tom.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:50.360
Wild waters, the ultimate gauntlet thrown down by nature.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:58.880
But for centuries, man has looked out there and also found a maritime muse.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:06.200
Inspiration for how we work with and survive nature at its most terrifying.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.480
What drives people to take on the elements?

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.480
And how do we contend with perilous seas?

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:27.960
I'm taking a journey down the north-east Coast of Northumberland on a quest to find out.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:38.280
Starting at windswept Bamburgh and heading for the mouth of the River Tyne.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:51.640
This coast is one of Britain's most hazardous, a 65 mile danger zone, with 780 known shipwrecks.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:05.960
'I'm venturing into these wild waters with RNLI coxswain, 'John Hanvey, to investigate why they've claimed so many victims.'

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.280
What is it, John, that makes this coast so dangerous?

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:28.280
Well, as you can see by looking at the chart, you have virtually a straight run up the coastline, and then you come across the Farne Islands, which the furthest one out is about three and a half miles, and because the Farne Islands are blocking the route of the tide, as it funnels through, the tide becomes very strong and runs in loads of different directions.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.960
So, it's like a gigantic, natural breakwater sticking out into the shipping lanes?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.560
It's like a millpond here today, John, but what's it like when you've got a really big storm blowing?

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:48.320
It's wild, got a lot of wind, a lot of tide, a lot of water coming over the top, the boat's rolling and when it's dark it makes it ten times worse.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:58.280
Another thing we get on this part of the coast is fog, and I think a lot of people who have been to sea all their life would sooner have a gale of wind than they would the fog.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.920
Because this coastline has a host of hidden hazards.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:18.680
Basically, between the Farnes, right down over Holy Island, we've got a lot of shallower water, we've got smaller islands, some of which have only got about a metre of water covered over the top of them, so if you don't know where they are, they would catch you out.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:24.600
These waters are so wild that even with modern navigation, vessels still sail into trouble.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:36.440
Two centuries ago, up to five ships a night were lost.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.440
What did they do before the days of the RNLI?

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.400
'They looked to a local landmark.'

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.520
Bamburgh Castle.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:51.080
I want to find out why it made this coast the cradle of life-saving.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.280
Bamburgh Castle is an impressive vantage point.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:06.280
300 years ago, it was home to Dr John Sharp, archdeacon and son of the Archbishop of York.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.560
He devoted his life to saving others.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:15.360
It's the late 1700s and Sharp is receiving weekly reports of drowned bodies being washed up on these shores.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:27.200
Sharp grows increasingly disturbed by the relentless loss of life and becomes obsessed with making our wild waters safer.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.520
'There was no nearby lighthouse, no ship-to-shore communication, 'no distress flares.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.480
'So, what did Sharp come up with?

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.640
'A set of instructions.'

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:44.120
It was a watch and rescue system.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:54.800
When the mists came down, or the waters turned wild, it was all eyes on the coast for those at Bamburgh Castle.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.240
He'd invented the first coastguard station.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:06.880
"In every great storm, 'two men on horseback are sent from the castle "to patrol from sunset to sunrise."

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.280
BELL RINGS "A bell on the south turret "will be rung out in every thick fog as a signal.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:18.760
"A person is to attend every morning to look out "if any ships be in distress."

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:24.200
If any were spotted, those at the castle jumped into action.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:32.320
First, a gun signalled the location of the wrecked ship.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.680
Prepare to give fire.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.040
One shot for the islands.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:41.520
Two for north.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:45.880
Three for south.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.400
But how could you communicate with stricken vessels?

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.760
Sharp's solution was a flag and a speaking trumpet.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.680
Ahoy there!

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.040
Help is at hand.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:05.040
Enormous iron chains hauled foundering ships to shore.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:22.480
The first coastguard at Bamburgh not only warned ships away from the coastline, but provided refuge for shipwrecked sailors, food, first aid, beds, space to store cargo.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:30.720
Despite Sharp's pioneering efforts, lives were still being lost.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.559
There was no slipway to get a boat out to stranded ships.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.360
He needed something remarkable.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:45.640
In 1788, Sharp contacted a London coach builder, Lionel Lukin.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:55.280
Lukin had just modified and patented a Norway yawl by adding air boxes, cork gunnels and a deeper keel to improve stability and buoyancy.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.560
Lukin called his vessel the 'unimmergible boat.'

00:08:54.680 --> 00:09:04.840
It was intended as a Thames work boat, but Sharp immediately saw its potential as a life-saver.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:17.400
How could a river boat from the placid Thames work in the wild waters of the North Sea?

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.120
'I'm meeting boat restorer, Tim West, to find out.'

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.120
Here it is.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.320
The coble.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.080
Specially designed for the north-east Coast.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:39.600
Because they're going into the water this way, the forefoot is a deep forefoot, so it acts as a rudder when you're going into the sea.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.600
When it's deep enough, they would then, they would then fit the rudder.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:49.920
They'd lean it over the stern and then drop the rudder into the, the pintles, aren't they, these holes here?

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.400
And then you've got a tiller somewhere.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:55.480
I do like model boats.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.400
Beautiful.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.080
So, that's what steers the boat.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.000
This coble could be launched from a flat beach.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:12.760
To stop the wild waters swallowing it, buoyant cork panels and air chambers were added.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.280
But was it really unsinkable?

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.000
'We're putting our scale model to the test.'

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:22.760
This is the North Sea.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:29.440
And we've managed to find two likely passengers in period dress.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:29.840
Very elegantly dressed, yeah.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:38.160
Lukin's principal design was to make sure the boat, even though it was fully overwhelmed, it wouldn't, er, it wouldn't completely sink.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.160
So, he fitted additional buoyancy in, which I've done here, with some cork covered with calico.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:49.800
Theoretically, if the boat was full of water, it wouldn't sink, even with survivors on board.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:49.920
Can we try that?

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.280
Here comes the North Sea storm.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.880
Right, here it comes.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:04.800
So, that's showing that the boat copes quite well in the sea, so let's er, let's see what happens when we submerge it.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.440
So, it doesn't sink, and it's not even turned upside down.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.480
So, Lupin's designed worked.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:20.720
So, the basic coble with extra buoyancy could stay afloat in a North Sea storm.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:20.840
Yep.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:31.320
That's remarkable, the beginning of the lifeboat story, demonstrated in a paddling pool on a beach at Bamburgh.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:36.440
John Sharp was a visionary who conquered these wild waters.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.720
He created the first coastguard and first lifeboat station.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:50.800
This hero of the waves gave rise to a crucial rescue service that still watches over our coast today.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:07.080
The coastguard is a staple of our seas.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:15.200
But a somewhat larger workhorse of our wild waters is the roll-on, roll-off ferry.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:23.840
Reliable and steadfast, it takes a pretty wild ocean to stop them sailing.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:30.760
These beasts of our maritime superhighway evolved from times of war.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:37.840
Now, they wage a daily battle to keep people and cargo moving.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:56.640
Departing from Stranraer on the west coast of Scotland, Mark's on a mission to investigate how 'RoRos' and their passengers stomach our wild waters.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:05.640
If you were going to design a ship to take on the high seas, a roll-on, roll-off ferry might not be the obvious choice.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:08.600
It's not, on first appearance, the most watertight of vessels.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:16.920
BEEPING RoRos have a huge, open stern, leading to a cavernous hull.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:26.520
If the water from here gets into here, it could be lethal.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:32.760
On the 31st of January, 1953, it did and it was.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:41.840
The wildest storm in living memory claimed the first RoRo casualty... ..the Princess Victoria.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:52.640
On this very route, Stranraer to Larne, her stern doors were ripped from their hinges, water flooded in.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:14:09.720
"Despite the valiant efforts of her crew "and other seafarers, "the Princess Victoria foundered "off the coast of Northern Ireland "with a loss of 133 lives."

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:15.280
An inquiry blamed wild waters and a fatal flaw in the stern door.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:20.000
It didn't provide a watertight seal for the car deck.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:37.160
Since then, failing doors and ferocious seas have been factors in two more European ferry sinkings, the 1987 Herald of Free Enterprise and The Estonia, less than a decade later.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:45.360
'I want to investigate what was learned from these disasters' and how ferries cope with stormy seas.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:56.480
Wild waters have been the driving force behind the science and safety of RoRo ferries.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:02.600
Whilst design hasn't changed, technology has.

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:07.120
Now these floating giants have CCTV everywhere.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:10.680
Sensors ensure doors are sealed shut.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:16.880
Checks between crew and bridge are constant before and during crossings.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:19.600
If anything is awry, a ship won't sail.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:27.520
But what happens if a ferry hits trouble out at sea?

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:33.680
Emergency drills are another product of lessons learnt.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:35.840
Ladies and gentlemen, may I have your attention, please.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:42.320
"This is the captain speaking, please listen very carefully."

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:42.880
All hands muster to emergency stations.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:52.720
Each week, crews from around our shores take part in emergency drills to evacuate passengers from these floating giants.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:55.040
Today, I'm going to join them.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:59.280
Go to the left, bear left and round the sink.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:02.120
Stay there and do your best.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:10.640
In an emergency at sea, passengers are instructed to find their nearest assembly station.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:14.400
Gosh, it's somewhere out here.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.000
First team, it's 10 minutes of air time.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:18.720
10 minutes of airtime left.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:28.080
Can you imagine what this would be like in a real emergency, finding your way round this labyrinth of doors?

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.760
The situation has now become very serious.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:34.400
As a result, I've decided to abandon ship.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.080
"Evacuate..."

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:39.240
'For the crew on board, 'this evacuation procedure is second nature.'

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.520
I think it's down here, outside decks.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:44.240
Chaps in orange.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.720
I feel safer now.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:55.600
There are four lifeboats on this ferry, each holding 125 people.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.960
And these people will help you onto the boat!

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:00.400
Please do what's instructed.

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:05.880
Walk on the boat, move on down and sit on the opposite side of the boat, please.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:10.560
There we are, safe.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.480
That was really very exciting.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:21.400
You know, these guys do it all the time, every Sunday morning, so they're so calm, but it was so quick, so fast.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:24.040
Evacuation at sea is thankfully rare.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:37.000
But there's one factor none of us can control on our wild waters... '..the great British weather.'

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:37.280
But can we be one step ahead of it?

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:41.920
'Paddy McAlinden is the ship's master.'

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:42.680
We have got much more detailed forecasts.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:50.480
As you can see here, there's everything in this, we've got sea states, wave heights, wind strengths, and they're planning this for every three hours, right through for a four-day sequence.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:58.520
Nowadays, you can really set your watch on the time of day a front going through, they are that accurate.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:01.960
Ultimately though, whether you sail or not is your decision as master?

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:12.240
My decision, the decision rests with me and if I say, "I do not consider the conditions favourable for the voyage", I will not sail.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:20.280
Just a tiny percentage of ferry crossings are cancelled due to bad weather.

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:28.040
The sheer size of RoRos means even when the waters are wild, you're probably in the most stable vessel on our seas.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:37.760
If you've got a big swell and, say, a small boat, you'll literally go up and down the full height of the waves, just like this - there you go.

00:18:38.120 --> 00:19:02.600
Now, if we've, on the other hand, got a RoRo ferry, the same swell... and that's going through the waves something like this... ..a funnel there... ..the vertical heave is much less, and therefore it's a much more comfortable ride.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.160
So, why do so many of us still get seasick?

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:20.000
Ferries can't avoid sideways roll - a key cause of nausea.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:26.360
On a RoRo the passengers are on higher decks than the cars for safety reasons.

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:41.840
Of course, that will mean it's much more nauseous up here than it is down here.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:59.320
Millions suffer seasickness, but what's going on inside our bodies when the nausea hits?

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:03.720
I'm putting myself on the line for science.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:03.880
Hi, John.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:07.200
Nice to meet you - and here's Rose, my assistant.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:15.080
Professor John Golding from the University of Westminster is an expert in motion sickness and spatial disorientation.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.280
So, can you make me seasick?

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:26.480
Well, we haven't got a boat here to actually do that, but we can mimic it by simply using a rotating chair.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:26.840
Right.

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:27.000
Here we go.

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:28.240
Sitting comfortably?

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:28.600
OK.

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:30.960
And Rose, if you could pass me the blindfold - do you mind wearing this blindfold?

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.280
No, that's all right.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:34.280
Now, I must warn you, I've never, ever been seasick.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:34.800
Right.

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:36.920
I've done lots of sailing, but never been seasick.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.360
Well, this is going to be a challenge.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.000
It is indeed.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:48.160
The reason we have the blindfold is to cut out your horizon view to make it a bit more difficult for you to resist motion sickness.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:50.040
And speed up a bit.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:50.240
Head up.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:55.240
Down.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.960
Hold it down.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.520
Up.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.120
Hold it.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:01.960
Down.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:08.800
I mean, are some people more susceptible to sea sickness than others?

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:09.200
Yes, there's huge differences.

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:11.160
It's partly genetics - it's also partly age.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:22.680
When you're young, about eight or nine years old, that's the most susceptible period of your life, then you get slightly more resistant as you get older, and women tend to be slightly more susceptible, as well.

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:37.080
Sea sickness is caused when the inner ear canals, which sense rotation spinning in the chair, conflict with the inner ear otoliths which sense acceleration, nodding the head.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.560
Beginning to feel ever so slightly nauseous.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.800
Head up, head down.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:40.360
Head up...

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:43.840
Yes, it's coming on now.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:44.120
..And head down.

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:44.440
Urgh...

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:49.120
Head up...and head down.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:50.280
Ugh.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:50.640
Eurgh...

00:21:53.320 --> 00:22:00.480
The sensory confusion tricks the body into thinking it's poisoned, which is why we're sick.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:07.040
That could be what we call the avalanche phenomenon, that things seem OK to begin with, and then it suddenly comes on.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:08.600
Oh, God.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:14.880
Well, just keep your head still and breathe.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:18.200
I have no idea where I am.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:18.480
Breathe - breathe slowly.

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:18.920
HE CHUCKLES Oh...

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:20.840
Oh, dear.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:26.560
I'm in good company, at least - even Nelson suffered sea sickness.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:39.640
Our bodies might bow to wild waters, but these titans of the waves have all the available technology to keep us safe from savage seas.

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:45.880
It doesn't take long to get your sea legs back, and suddenly I feel very much in control.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.920
Turning that to the centre.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:47.680
That's the thrusters?

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:50.120
The thrusters, now, are back controlled here.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:56.240
I'm about to do something that, as a sailor, I've always wanted.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:56.720
Oh, it's so exciting!

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.160
OK then, Mark, push the handles right down.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:22:59.520
Right to the end?

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:01.000
Right down.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:05.600
Yeah, you've done it there.

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:11.680
I've taken the helm of a RoRo out into our wild waters.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:28.600
Unforgiving seas are not for the faint-hearted.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:40.960
Some hardened seafarers spend most of their life in wild waters... sea birds.

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:50.720
But there is one patch of solid ground to which they flock - the Farne Islands.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:24:06.920
28 rocky bastions standing steadfast against swell and tide.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:15.000
Wildlife photographer Richard Taylor-Jones has come to this inaccessible idyll at a wild time of the year.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:19.720
It's June.

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:42.120
The Farnes play host to 140,000 arctic terns... shags... puffins... guillemots... and eiders - the male our most handsome, heaviest and fastest flying duck... ..the female just a brown bundle.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:55.480
These feathered seafarers have worked out how to do everything at sea, except breed.

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:55.920
For that, they come ashore.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:25:03.000
A carpet of copulation swarming and swirling for a few short weeks to produce their young.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:09.840
Why choose the Farnes?

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:22.040
Quite simply, the islands are free of ground-dwelling predators - rats, cats, dogs, foxes - kept away by the wild sea.

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:32.120
But ironically, safety on land isn't guaranteed for eider ducklings.

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:38.520
They leave their nests as soon as they've hatched and make for the sea.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:48.400
Food will not be brought to them - it's move or starve.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:54.360
Hungry eyes watch and wait.

00:25:54.680 --> 00:26:00.880
Herring gulls have a snack in mind.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.520
For four hours, this eider duck mother is cornered...

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:16.320
..but one chick escapes her watchful eye.

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:33.640
It's now easy prey.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:52.200
Not all stay safe on the Farnes, but most ducklings will reach the sea.

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:58.040
Soon this busy, bustling world will empty.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.800
The birds will head back out into wild waters.

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:32.080
The power of the sea sculpts the coast... ..bearing witness to tragedy... and triumph.

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:40.840
I'm exploring one of Britain's most treacherous coasts - the North East.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:48.160
Wild waters awash with feats of conquest and survival.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:53.600
I've reached Seahouses Harbour.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:28:06.760
Getting in here takes some nifty navigation... ..and the help of the Longstone Lighthouse, six miles offshore.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:14.360
But two centuries ago it was the lighthouse keeper's daughter who became a leading light in the world of life-saving.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:19.360
Come to this coast and you can't miss Grace Darling.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:27.240
In 1838 she spotted the steamship Forfarshire through the window of the lighthouse.

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:35.720
Reports at the time described the seas as being "lashed by the tempest into the most tumultuous commotion."

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:36.240
The ship was broken in two - one half lodging on the rocks.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:46.360
Grace and her father rowed into the eye of the storm and rescued nine survivors - and Grace became a national heroine.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:57.120
The splendidly named Society for the Recovery of Persons Apparently Drowned awarded Grace a specially minted medal.

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:03.040
On it, a cherub blowing life into the dying embers of a torch - a metaphor for saving life.

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:17.680
It coincided with a burgeoning interest in the science of resuscitation... ..but, 200 years ago, how did they set about bringing the drowned back to life?

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:20.400
Hello, Craig.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:24.520
Craig Barclay is a local historian.

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:34.800
The technique which probably seems strangest to us in the 21st century is the tobacco enema, and the key to that is the bellows.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.640
That sounds incredibly painful - how did it work?

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:39.280
Essentially, imagine me with my pipe of tobacco.

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:44.720
Imagine you have just been dragged out of the harbour - life appears extinct.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:57.040
My solution to this problem is to take a long drag on my pipe, to blow the smoke through a rubber tube into the bellows and to insert these bellows into your backside and blow.

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:59.080
I think I'd probably jump clean back into the water again.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:06.720
Not very much fun, but it was felt that tobacco would stimulate you back into life.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:09.960
Blowing smoke rings.

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:14.360
If you were unlucky enough to drown in a spot without a handy rectal fumigation kit, fear not - other methods were available.

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:19.760
The barrel roll, to force air in and out of the chest.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:24.880
And the trotting horse method, to compress the chest and bounce the body to restore breathing.

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:40.120
Harbours like this were the front line in the fight-back against wild waters.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:40.520
There was just one catch.

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:41.680
These imaginative methods weren't very effective.

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:48.160
Half a century later, new guidelines were published.

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:51.640
Instruction manuals such as this were in circulation.

00:30:50.680 --> 00:31:01.320
They said you should never hang someone upside-down, you should never roll them on a barrel you should never blow tobacco into their backside.

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:02.680
I imagine a round of applause, probably, from certain sea-goers.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:05.600
I think a round of applause from many.

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:22.760
The techniques recommended continued to be warming the body, massaging the body, but also inserting air into the body - again using the bellows, but now inserted into the nostril with the other nostril sealed, mouth sealed, and once you had a good seal you could use that to inflate the lungs.

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:27.280
So a first step towards modern resuscitation.

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:46.560
It may have taken some eye-watering experimentation to get to our modern world of defibrillators and oxygen masks, but the principle of resuscitation remains unchanged - getting air back into our lungs.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:32:09.440
On our coast, nature has the power to take your breath away... ..but some people find the wildest water below the surface.

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:24.600
Under the waves lurks an untamed world of watery wildlife.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:26.120
In Dublin Bay, Miranda's preparing to take the plunge.

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:43.400
Here, these seemingly tranquil waters have a wild side... and come with a sting in their tail.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:54.720
Dublin Bay is a Mecca for open sea swimmers - they come here every weekend to flex their muscles against tide and temperature.

00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:56.880
Four minutes.

00:32:56.040 --> 00:32:58.720
But these wild waters aren't just home to aquatic athletes.

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:07.520
Out there is Britain's most venomous jellyfish, the lion's mane - and I'm about to enter its lair.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:17.360
The lion's mane can grow up to two metres in diameter, and its tentacles trail over an area of 500 square metres.

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:22.840
It's one of the largest jellyfish in the world.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:27.600
This king of the underwater jungle feeds on plankton and all other jellyfish.

00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:33.520
Many swimmers have had painful encounters on this coast.

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:35.200
So, how did it feel?

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:36.000
What was it like?

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.640
Nasty.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:45.520
I mean, there's an absolute sudden, um, just stinging pain, and it was really, really intense.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:53.800
It was kind of like a burn and sting at the same time, really, really strong - like, I just literally screamed in the water, I was like, "Whoa!"

00:33:52.960 --> 00:34:03.960
I tried everything - I tried whisky on it and then whisky in me, I tried urine, I tried vinegar, I tried the whole shebang, and it was torture.

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:07.320
They say alcohol is very good.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.560
What, to drink it?

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:20.679
As the swimmers set off hoping for a sting-free race, I want to know what attracts these venomous jellyfish to this coast... and why they're so hazardous.

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:22.120
Good morning.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:24.920
How you doing, you all right?

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:36.679
I'm heading out with Dr Tom Doyle of the National University of Ireland, Galway and Damien Haberlin from University College Cork.

00:34:36.199 --> 00:34:40.800
They're trying to unlock the secrets of the lion's mane in a bid to find an antidote.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:45.239
What is it about this area that they really like?

00:34:44.320 --> 00:35:05.080
There seems to be some sort of a retaining feature, and the jellyfish likes that kind of habitat, where basically they're moving back and forth with the tide, they're not being swept out on the ocean currents, and a lot of jellyfish, they do actually like to stay in a particular area, so there'll be a lot of other jellyfish species here that they'd actually feed on.

00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:10.000
There's actually two.

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:10.400
Yeah!

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:13.720
And there's a blue jelly, as well.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:23.040
Lion's manes are relatively weak swimmers, but congregate in favourable conditions.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:27.360
So, how do you study a potentially lethal jellyfish?

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:28.200
Get in these wild waters and catch one.

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:40.600
Cue wet suits... ..and a liberal application of petroleum jelly.

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:42.440
I've never taken so many precautions going into the water before.

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:45.920
That feels disgusting.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:48.520
I feel like I've trowelled it on.

00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:52.040
You're looking good, there, actually.

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:55.400
Suited and booted, it's time to sit and wait.

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:01.840
OK there's one there, there's one there, go there.

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:09.480
Is that definitely a lion's mane?

00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:10.640
Yeah, it's definitely a lion's mane.

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:11.000
Oh, that's good.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:16.920
Going to scoop it up.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:21.320
I'm going to stay back a bit - those tentacles look really long!

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.640
Our catch is a mere baby.

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:26.840
Great stuff, well done.

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:32.760
Picking up a fully grown specimen would be highly dangerous.

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:33.160
And there we go.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:35.680
Oh, that's a good one - look at the tentacles, wow.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:36.000
Really sticky!

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:39.680
Plenty of venom.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:46.320
I think I've been stung, actually.

00:36:45.680 --> 00:36:49.840
A bit like a nettle sting, I think, just up here.

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:53.800
Yeah, yeah - it looks red, so it looks like you've been stung, all right.

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:54.560
Yeah.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:55.240
Yeah, OK.

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:56.200
Hardcore.

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:59.440
You can join the team now.

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:00.560
Thank you!

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:07.080
So, I've obviously been stung by a very small bit of tentacle - what reaction is happening inside my body?

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:22.000
Each tentacle has thousands and thousands of stinging capsules like a balloon, and inside that is coiled up, effectively, a harpoon - these balloons burst, that fires the harpoon that pierces your skin and then the venom is injected into your system.

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:30.520
Once inside, scientists think - based on other jellies - that venom attaches on to red blood cells, making a hole which releases potassium.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:42.040
A serious sting with large amounts of venom could cause a dangerous flush of potassium, whose effects the scientists are still investigating.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:51.000
Developing an antidote means collecting the venom by taking off some toxic tentacles.

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:55.640
Snip.

00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:59.240
So, trimming those tentacles off - is that actually harming the jellyfish?

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:07.880
No, these animals lose tentacles all the time, when they capture prey or when they get entangled in seaweed, or anything like that.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:09.480
We can see them floating around in the water.

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:09.800
Yeah, absolutely, so, you know, taking some tentacles...

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.360
It's just like a haircut.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:12.160
It's just like a haircut, yeah.

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:13.280
All right.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:18.920
This sample will be analysed in a laboratory.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:29.760
The scientists hope their work here will not only help them understand stings, but could lead to successful antivenom for the lion's mane.

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:38.880
The very wildness of these waters lures us, as well as these beautiful, captivating creatures.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:46.920
Creatures we're one step closer to understanding, thanks to the swimmers and the scientists.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:59.960
We're exploring the wild waters of our coast.

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:18.240
From vast expanses of open seas... ..to epic oceans, whose waves crash on our coast.

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:24.760
The lure of our wild waters is irresistible.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:40.800
I'm traversing the treacherous tides of the North East, on a mission to uncover how man contends with nature to save lives.

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:48.320
I've reached Tynemouth... ..a seemingly calm river-mouth with deadly obstacles.

00:39:47.440 --> 00:39:58.960
Here, shallow seas meet with shifting sands and hidden rocks known ominously as the Black Middens.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:03.760
This treacherous topography is a natural wrecking zone.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:12.720
Rescue by lifeboat is difficult enough... ..so, can you rise above it?

00:40:13.640 --> 00:40:23.880
This is a breeches buoy - basically a life buoy attached to the top part of a pair of trousers.

00:40:23.120 --> 00:40:36.160
A line was fired from the shore to the sinking ship, or the wreck, and then one at a time survivors climbed into the breeches buoy and were hauled to safety by the onshore team.

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:44.960
Good in theory, but what I want to know is how it worked in reality, and why this antiquated aid is still being used on this part of the coast.

00:40:46.520 --> 00:41:08.080
Invented in 1808, the breeches buoy was once commonplace on our coast... ..but it was never more needed here than in 1864, when the Black Middens claimed three ships in one dark night.

00:41:07.120 --> 00:41:15.840
The local coastguard couldn't operate the breeches buoy quickly enough.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:18.800
Watching was one John Morrison.

00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:26.840
Realising trained backup could have saved lives, he founded the first Volunteer Life Brigade.

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:33.640
They're one of just two remaining teams in Britain trained in this ship-to-shore rescue system.

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:36.560
But how much graft does it take to save lives?

00:41:35.720 --> 00:41:44.160
In my quest to find out first-hand how the breeches buoy works, I've volunteered to be rescued.

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:57.200
I'm heading out to my 19th century shipwreck, where the stricken crew include the local vicar, who's trying to make the best of a bad situation.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:42:02.720
Eternal Father, creator of land and sea, we pray for all those involved in the life...

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:08.920
Today's drill is for display purposes, but it's still an essential part of Brigade training.

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:14.400
Tynemouth Volunteer Life Brigade, action!

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:24.160
A rocket fires a line from land to boat.

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:24.680
Fire!

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:33.720
No mean feat in wild waters.

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:36.760
The breeches buoy is then hauled across to the shipwreck.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.160
Here we go - it's my turn.

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:42.440
And now my fate is in the hands of the Brigade.

00:42:46.760 --> 00:43:07.560
This is just a drill, but it's exciting enough to give me a flavour of what it would be like being rescued from a sinking ship in a raging storm - it would be frightening.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:12.440
It's surprising how the cold of the water takes your breath away.

00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:19.080
I've got very little energy for saving myself - I'm entirely in the hands of the Brigade.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:21.120
Little short bites, guys, come on!

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:29.440
It takes the team less than two minutes to drag me the hundred metres to safety.

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:31.800
Thank you, thank you.

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:44.920
I'm breathless, and I've done nothing but been dragged through the water - at the most incredible speed, it's far faster than I expected.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:48.520
But there's no rest for me.

00:43:47.720 --> 00:43:54.280
Now I'm turning rescuer, and joining the Brigade to haul in the next man.

00:43:53.360 --> 00:44:00.680
My arms are burning.

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:06.560
Keep going, guys, come on.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:07.360
Little short bites, little short bites.

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:16.840
This team have developed their own language - every command practised until its second nature.

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:21.560
The water's pulling it...

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:22.720
Keep a hand on the rope at all times.

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:25.960
Do you feel the shackle coming?

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:26.640
Tell the guy behind you, please.

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:26.800
Shackle!

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.080
Shackle!

00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:27.560
Shackle!

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:27.840
Shackle!

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:34.120
Fine, how are you?!

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.560
Oh!

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:35.080
Oh!

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:38.000
My arms are like overcooked spaghetti.

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:42.160
I've got nothing below the shoulders.

00:44:41.240 --> 00:44:49.800
I've only hauled in the vicar - these volunteers will do this ten times.

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:53.400
Twice for each rescue.

00:44:52.480 --> 00:45:02.400
The breeches buoy is rarely used today, but the teamwork it demands has remained essential for overcoming our wild waters.

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:06.960
Being part of this team and sharing their camaraderie for a short while helps to wash away fears of the ocean and its violent moods.

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:19.280
This band of brothers are testament to the courage of Tyneside's volunteers.

00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:33.520
Courage is often called for on our coast.

00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:41.920
Our waters are at their wildest during times of war.

00:45:44.920 --> 00:45:53.720
One narrow stretch has been contested for centuries - the English Channel.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:56.880
During the Second World War, plans were afoot to invade.

00:45:56.200 --> 00:46:05.760
Hitler's eyes were fixed on our white cliffs.

00:46:04.920 --> 00:46:09.560
This coast was renamed Hellfire Corner.

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:25.240
Dick Strawbridge is investigating how these wild waters made front page news, when they were put to work to extinguish the enemy threat.

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:34.880
The Second World War has thrown up many strange stories.

00:46:34.160 --> 00:46:40.760
Rumours are spread fastest when Britain has faced her greatest threats.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:52.040
But one tale persists to this day... the story that German troops actually invaded the south coast in 1940.

00:46:51.160 --> 00:47:06.800
This headline on the 15th of December 1940 claims that 80,000 charred German bodies were washed up on British beaches after a failed invasion attempt - they'd been consumed by fire.

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:16.720
Now, I'm an ex-military man, I've never heard of this - but this isn't any local rag, this is the New York Times.

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:20.200
What was this "all-consuming fire" off our shores?

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:30.320
If it claimed 80,000 victims and foiled an invasion, why isn't this episode of wartime history something we all know?

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:36.360
It's quiet and quaint now, but 75 years ago, this was Britain's front line.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:46.440
I want to uncover the truth about the fiery hell of these waters and the secret wartime weapon that supposedly stopped a German invasion.

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:59.160
By May 1940, the Germans had taken and occupied Belgium, Holland and France - just 20 miles away.

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:06.960
Most of Britain's weapons had been left behind on the beaches of Dunkirk.

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:07.280
Our Channel coast was a sitting duck.

00:48:06.400 --> 00:48:20.120
We installed some obvious defences - pillboxes, tunnels, gun emplacements... but were we really going to use all-consuming fire to scupper the enemy?

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:22.760
One man believed we could.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:28.600
Lord Hankey, a man obsessed with developing weapons of fire.

00:48:28.560 --> 00:48:34.560
Lord Hankey was the head of the top secret Petroleum Warfare Department.

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:41.040
His classical education sparked an interest in using fire to fight the enemy.

00:48:40.280 --> 00:48:48.560
Hankey knew the 7th century Byzantines built ships that spewed out flames to form a fiery floating barrier on the sea.

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:55.240
It inspired his own idea for an impenetrable defence - setting the Channel on fire.

00:48:54.360 --> 00:48:58.560
But to make fire, you need this stuff - fuel.

00:49:00.160 --> 00:49:07.200
By 1940, Britain had squirreled away a surplus of it.

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:14.160
As the invasion threat loomed, Lord Hankey was determined to put it to use.

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:21.760
But could Hankey pull off his audacious plan to defend our coast with a fence of fire?

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:28.760
I know fuel floats on water, but how do you get it to burn?

00:49:28.040 --> 00:49:31.280
David, lovely to meet you.

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:39.160
To find out, I've enlisted chemist Dr David Kinnison from Southampton University.

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:43.600
Is it as simple as pouring oil on water and setting it on fire, because oil floats?

00:49:43.640 --> 00:49:44.920
No, it just won't burn.

00:49:43.960 --> 00:49:46.040
Why doesn't it light?

00:49:45.120 --> 00:49:54.320
It requires a lot of heat, because there's not enough vapour there that can mix with air to ignite.

00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:57.160
So, what do we do?

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:59.160
Well, what we can do is introduce some lighter fuels - for example, something like petrol.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:03.280
Right, petrol, we know petrol you put a match anywhere near it, it goes off - but does it burn on water?

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:06.240
Yes, it does, and the reason it will burn is because it's highly volatile, very easy to light.

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:12.800
It's not the actual petrol, it's the vapour above it.

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:13.120
Indeed, that is exactly it.

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:18.200
That does go "whoof", doesn't it?

00:50:17.360 --> 00:50:19.280
Yeah.

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.920
So, as a solution to your defences?

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:22.080
This will not last - it's over very quickly, your defence is lost.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:24.320
So, how do we turn this into a defensive weapon, then?

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:31.840
If we add heavier oils, these will last longer and we can get a sustained burn.

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:36.520
Right, OK.

00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:47.320
What's happening now, there's this petrol - as it burns, it generates heat, and that heat is absorbed by the oils and they will start to vaporise and burn as well.

00:50:46.360 --> 00:50:47.640
That is burning a lot more ferociously.

00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:48.080
Yep.

00:50:49.240 --> 00:50:52.840
In fact, the water's bubbling.

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:53.280
Yep.

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:54.440
Wow.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:59.880
That's the actual water turning into steam and bubbling out.

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:01.400
Yeah.

00:51:00.560 --> 00:51:03.000
The sea on fire.

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:13.320
Our experiment demonstrates how a flame barrage could work in principle, but the wild waters of the Channel are a different challenge altogether.

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:16.200
So, did Hankey really do it?

00:51:15.280 --> 00:51:20.920
Hello, hello!

00:51:20.160 --> 00:51:27.560
Brothers John and Paul Stone were there the day the sea caught fire.

00:51:27.840 --> 00:51:33.960
We lived just at the top of the hill, and you'd see this curtain of black smoke come up.

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:40.720
I can remember Mum and Dad saying, "Oh, they're testing the oil defences down in the bay."

00:51:40.920 --> 00:51:47.040
The whole line of black smoke all the way across the bay.

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:58.520
It was so dense and sticky and black that they'd actually discoloured the White Cliffs of Dover, so our white cliffs became grey cliffs for quite a few years afterwards.

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:06.960
John and Paul had witnessed one of the first flame barrage experiments.

00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:18.920
The tests worked - they were even filmed, so scientists could study the weapon and make adjustments.

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:23.640
This was fire on an unprecedented scale - just imagine it.

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:35.280
We've got flames 40 feet in the air, black, noxious smoke coming up, covering the beach, even going over the cliffs.

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:43.680
It was impressive - but was it a practical proposition?

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:49.280
Was this defence system ever rolled out along our coast, and if so, how?

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:59.360
To find out, I've come a few miles north where local historian Stuart Smith made an incredible discovery.

00:52:58.400 --> 00:53:03.720
A little prized possession of mine.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:07.280
It's part of the flame barrage?

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:15.240
It's quite simple, really - cos when I pulled it out, it actually still smelt of the petroleum, the mixture.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:16.560
There's a very slight whiff to it, but...

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:19.440
Unfortunately it's been exposed to the air now, but when it...

00:53:19.480 --> 00:53:24.920
Yeah, but actually you can see, it's all black and tarry inside.

00:53:24.040 --> 00:53:27.840
This is an amazing piece of history, isn't it?

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:30.360
Oh, that's why I love it.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:30.680
But where did this go to?

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:38.280
The hotel there, The Clarendon Hotel, and when they were digging the new wall that's along here, they actually came across the pipes.

00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:39.960
Now, I'm lucky enough to have the photograph, still.

00:53:39.240 --> 00:53:43.040
This is the exact spot - you can see everything's still the same.

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:44.200
Yeah, this is the exact spot.

00:53:43.480 --> 00:53:44.680
How did they get the fuel out into the sea?

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:49.320
Well, they had pumps down in the cellar of the hotel.

00:53:48.920 --> 00:54:00.920
They built two massive tanks at the back and it pumped across here, and they say that the pumps were so powerful that they could pump the mixture up to a mile.

00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:04.040
Why would they have it in a pub?

00:54:03.200 --> 00:54:07.280
To hide it - pure camouflage.

00:54:06.680 --> 00:54:10.600
I've never been in a pub where petroleum, not pints, once ran through its pipes.

00:54:09.640 --> 00:54:19.040
And if you look through here, you can actually see where the pipes came in, and they're all still there.

00:54:18.080 --> 00:54:23.440
This would have been the heart of the whole system, and it all would have happened here.

00:54:22.480 --> 00:54:34.240
I mean, the Germans were coming, the Battle of Britain was raging above our heads in the skies, and the men were desperate for a defence - and this is it.

00:54:34.840 --> 00:54:36.840
The plan was to defend key beaches from Kent to Dorset.

00:54:35.960 --> 00:54:40.880
There were flame barrages installed in Studland Bay...

00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:55.160
..Rye... ..key beaches around Dover... all the way up to Sandwich.

00:54:56.440 --> 00:55:01.600
So, did we fire up this barrage and stop a German invasion in 1940?

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:16.320
We know the sea caught fire... ..there's physical proof the barrage was installed... ..but there's still no evidence that the Germans invaded... and no trace of 80,000 charred bodies.

00:55:15.680 --> 00:55:17.960
This headline is all I've got.

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:21.080
Surely there's no smoke without fire?

00:55:20.120 --> 00:55:30.960
Historian Lee Richards has studied how fact and fiction merge in the fog of war.

00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:34.760
There certainly would be charred bodies in the Channel.

00:55:33.880 --> 00:55:47.160
At the time, the Germans were doing practice drills, getting incendiary bombs thrown on them, aircrew being shot down, naval engagements - so, there would be lots of bodies in the Channel.

00:55:46.200 --> 00:55:51.600
So there were some charred bodies, but not on the same scale?

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:54.840
People were coming to the wrong conclusions.

00:55:54.080 --> 00:56:01.400
Britain had formed something called the Underground Propaganda Committee...

00:56:00.440 --> 00:56:01.920
Right.

00:56:01.040 --> 00:56:03.360
..And their job was to exaggerate Britain's military potential.

00:56:02.600 --> 00:56:13.800
They'd come up with a rumour that there had been an invasion, it had been defeated by this British secret weapon that sets the sea on fire.

00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:17.880
It got into the foreign press, it was then picked up by the British press.

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:27.480
So, the information was being passed out to different... and coming back into our press to be spread as news, even though we started the rumour.

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:30.440
Absolutely correct, yes.

00:56:29.600 --> 00:56:42.240
Leaflets were also printed to warn the Germans that invading Britain would be unwise, and dropped over enemy lines.

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:47.360
Here we have one that helped to spread the story of the burning sea.

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:47.840
Ah!

00:56:48.360 --> 00:56:50.000
It's giving them phrases that they would find useful during their invasion.

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:58.880
It has such phrases as, "I can smell oil on the sea", "Look, the water's burning", "Look at the captain, he's burning beautifully".

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:04.280
It's really quite a graphic message - "Come to England, we will burn you and you will end up as ashes."

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:05.680
Yes.

00:57:04.800 --> 00:57:07.840
This propaganda - did it have a positive effect?

00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:11.840
Here we are, you believe that we can set the sea on fire - did it affect the Germans, do you think?

00:57:10.960 --> 00:57:14.480
I mean, it's certainly detrimental to German morale...

00:57:13.520 --> 00:57:15.120
But good for our morale.

00:57:15.480 --> 00:57:33.520
Yes, as I say, perhaps more importantly, it was good for the morale of Britain, it was good for the morale of the occupied countries, it was telling the neutral countries, particularly America, that we were still in the fight, we were still strong, and we had the potential to win.

00:57:34.760 --> 00:57:37.840
A year would elapse between the headline in the New York Times and the Americans joining the war.

00:57:37.480 --> 00:57:42.920
In 1940, the flame barrage was our winning weapon.

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:56.960
A weapon that had once petrified the enemy, yet reassured Britain and her allies - a weapon whose existence was enough.

00:57:56.160 --> 00:57:59.960
It never actually needed to be used.

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:13.640
Whether it was the fire on the sea or the firing of imaginations, wild rumours generated in this part of the coast were actually one of Britain's secret weapons during the Second World War.

00:58:22.160 --> 00:58:29.440
Our wild waters have been both barrier... ..and gateway.

00:58:31.080 --> 00:58:44.480
Pitching ourselves against nature makes for thrilling spectacle... ..and spectacular stories.

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:47.280
Wild waters test our mettle.

00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:54.400
Contending with them demands resourcefulness, resilience, respect.

00:58:54.320 --> 00:58:58.960
Not only do they shape our coast, but also our coastal character.

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:22.760
If you think rush hour on Britain's railways is busy, try Mumbai.

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:34.000
They call it "super dense crush load".

00:59:33.040 --> 00:59:34.160
It's a fight!

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:34.840
What is happening?

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:40.280
The one thing I'm not worried about is falling over.

00:59:46.520 --> 01:00:09.320
I'm literally not on my feet.
